I have two solutions:

<a> Perfectly O(1)

<b> Some logarithmic function of n

----------------------------------------------------
<a> Perfectly O(1)

Each time you realloc grow by an exponential size capped by a max. If the initial array and growth is below 4k 
dont waste my time just do a flat copy end of discussion. Of course this assumes you can CAS the pointer to the 
new location so add should take in **array not *array. search/remove can continue to use *array.

If its larger than 4k then remap old page(s) to new virtual memory location where you find the new spot and allocate
fresh zero pages for those beyond. Simple and end of story .. no copy required.

What happens if you cant find contiguous vmem location? Well then OOME what else :-)

This is where Java comes in handy with its copy collection and full GC memory compaction!! 

----------------------------------------------------
<b> Some logarithmic function of n

Same trick as above use **Array for add and *Array for search/remove. Embed size in either log base or multiples of some 
constant as part of the pointer.  Add a next pointer to end of this block. Thats it!!

When you are asked for an index that is not in your chunk follow next pointer `n' times. Really a glorified Linked List!!


